## std::sync — thread / Mutex / Arc / SharedSync surface (partial).
##
## Wave 2 (#06.8) — partial migration: all 10 class shells moved
## here (Thread, ThreadId, JoinHandle[T: Send], Mutex[T],
## MutexGuard[T], Arc[T], SharedSync[T], PoisonError, ThreadPanic,
## plus Context / Waker from std::task). Methods on every shell
## still flow through the runtime_table mangled-name dispatch
## (`Thread_spawn` → `riven_thread_spawn`, …) until T#21 lands.
##
## The `ThreadId` *value-scope* alias (so you can write `ThreadId`
## as a sentinel value, not just as a type) stays in Rust — it's a
## one-line `DefKind::Variable` shim in `resolve/stdlib/mod.rs` and
## moves naturally only when class-body lib blocks land for the
## constants that need it. The std.sync module's items list keeps
## that shim's DefId alongside the bootstrap-loaded class DefIds
## via the APPEND semantics of `fixup_bootstrapped_stdlib_modules`.
##
## SharedSync[T] is the Ruby-naming canonical name (TEC-13 / §10a)
## and lives here in self-hosted .rvn. `Arc[T]` is preserved as a
## backward-compat alias class in Rust: its `type_constructors`
## Variable is typed `Ty::Class { name: "SharedSync" }` so
## `Arc.new(5)` returns `Ty::Class { name: "SharedSync", … }` and
## `let x: SharedSync[Int] = Arc.new(5)` typechecks cleanly. Both
## import paths (`use std.sync.SharedSync` AND `use std.sync.Arc`)
## still resolve.
##
## Context / Waker belong to std::task (async runtime plumbing) but
## live here in `sync.rvn` for now because the standalone task.rvn
## module hasn't been split out — they are still indexed under
## `std.sync.{Context, Waker}` per the legacy registration.

class Context
end

class Waker
end

class Thread
end

class ThreadId
end

class JoinHandle[T: Send]
end

class Mutex[T]
end

class MutexGuard[T]
end

class SharedSync[T]
end

class PoisonError
end

class ThreadPanic
end
